 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 3,
   "plaque": "falklands_camp",
   "title": "Camp",
   "text": "From here the land belongs to Camp: moorland, tussac grass, peat banks and sheep farms scattered along the coast. A settlement may have a dozen people and a shop, and the next may be a day's drive of rough track away. In the old days everyone went by horse. Most people go by Land Rover, and by light aircraft."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 12,
   "plaque": "falklands_gypsy",
   "title": "Gypsy Cove",
   "text": "The cove east of Stanley where Magellanic penguins burrow in the sandy banks above the beach. They come ashore in September to breed, and bray like donkeys, which is why the islanders call them jackass penguins. The beaches here were mined in 1982, and the last mines were cleared across the islands in 2020."
  },
  {
   "x": 54,
   "y": 8,
   "plaque": "falklands_volunteer",
   "title": "Volunteer Point",
   "text": "At the north-east tip of East Falkland is the biggest colony of king penguins in the islands, with gentoo and Magellanic penguins nearby. The kings stand a metre tall, orange-necked, in a crowd that looks like a pub on pay day. The track out here is rough, and worth it."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "falklands_wildlife",
   "title": "Wildlife in Camp",
   "text": "The Falklands have five kinds of penguin, elephant seals, sea lions, and Commerson's dolphins that play in the surf. The islands are home to birds seen almost nowhere else, such as the striated caracara, called Johnny rook, which will investigate your sandwiches, your rucksack and the rest of your day."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "falklands_peat_banks",
   "title": "The Peat Banks",
   "text": "Peat cutters work in summer, slicing blocks from the bank with a special spade. The blocks dry in stacks, then go to the house by trailer. A season's fuel for one home is hundreds of blocks. It is hard and wet work, and the best part of the day is the tea."
  },
  {
   "x": 56,
   "y": 20,
   "plaque": "falklands_tussac",
   "title": "Tussac Grass",
   "text": "Tussac grass grows in huge clumps up to two metres tall along the coast, and once covered most of the islands' shores. Sheep and fires ate much of it. It shelters seabirds, seals and penguins. Wherever it is left, so is the wildlife that depends on it."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 50,
   "plaque": "falklands_warrah",
   "title": "The Warrah",
   "text": "The Falkland Islands wolf, or warrah, was the islands' only native land mammal, a fox-like animal with a white tail tip. Charles Darwin saw one in 1833 and noted that it was tame and curious. It was hunted and poisoned, and the last was killed in about 1876. It is one of very few canids known to have gone extinct in historic times."
  },
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 36,
   "plaque": "falklands_diddle",
   "title": "Diddle-Dee",
   "text": "Diddle-dee is a low red-berried heath that covers the hillsides of the islands, turning the Camp a rusty brown in autumn. The berries are made into jam, pies and a sort of cordial, and are one of the Falkland tastes."
  }
 ],
